A1. All in a lifetime. ~ BIOGRAPHY
A9. With some Scotch in him, he is only happy when he can lick someone. ~ TERRIER
A10. If you don't like this one, make it over. ~ OER
A12. This is rather old-fashioned for you. ~ THEE
A13. This promises well for the future. ~ NOTE
A15. What she must do to every stitch she has. ~ SEW
A17. What they found in their alphabet soup in a Greek restaurant. ~ PHI
A18. What they wished his speech would do for humanity's sake. ~ END
A20. This verb is compound. ~ MIX
A22. This used to be a sporty place in Rome. ~ ARENA
A25. This could be a delightful tune. ~ FOR
A27. What some people like to put on when they go stepping out (slang of the '90's). ~ AGONY
A29. A filling station. ~ REFECTORY
A31. The one that makes the biggest hit in Congress. ~ GAVEL
A32. A boyish expression. ~ AW
A33. A face brush. ~ BEARD
A34. A native of Ohio. ~ TOLEDO
A35. What you will have to do when you paddle your own canoe. ~ STEER
A37. She let this drop at the breakfast table. ~ REMARK
A42. Those who stand in with the elevator boy always get this. ~ RAISE
A45. We are giving you the doctor's word for this. ~ AH
A46. You might find this one in a bag of marbles. ~ AGATE
A47. A gangster. ~ DESPERADO
A49. What they did when they opened up the bank. ~ PRIED
A50. Poetical thoughts. ~ ODE
A51. You may need this if you expect to play the second fiddle. ~ RESIN
A52. One line that continues to go down and has not reached bottom yet. ~ HEM
A53. Here is old Ra himself in a Roman garb. ~ SOL
A55. What the farmer must do to his own row this year. ~ HOE
A56. A calling Theodore Roosevelt wished one of his sons to follow. ~ TED
A58. This one loves his dog. ~ FLEA
A61. These have been monkeying around for a long time. ~ APES
A63. Lady, deer Lady. ~ DOE
A65. What Col. Lindbergh always does at home after returning from a flying trip. ~ ARRIVES
A67. This is a novel affair. ~ CHARACTER

D1. You might get a shock if you punched this buzzer. ~ BEE
D2. When you have this at breakfast it is apt to spoil your dinner. ~ IRE
D3. The best connection we could make for this. ~ OR
D4. The kick that is found in ginger-ale. ~ GIN
D5. This auto make a good ear. ~ REO
D6. This comes right from the heart. ~ ARTERY
D7. The house will always stand back of this (abbr.). ~ HO
D8. "A cheerful little earful" that comes from a negative attitude. ~ YES
D9. This is apt to take your last cent. ~ THIEF
D11. When they do this to the lame ducks it will take some of the quack out of the bills. ~ REMOVE
D12. After a long dispute this is what they did to china. ~ THREW
D14. If a printer puts one good tire on this it will be complete in all parts. ~ EN
D16. Paid tribute to Bacchus. ~ WINED
D17. This subdivision was started with a large capital. ~ PARAGRAPH
D19. This means Tony. ~ DAGO
D21. A striking sound effect on the radio. ~ XYLOPHONE
D23. We will give you a tip for this one. ~ NEB
D24. Ocean travelers say this is the one spot on the deck. ~ ACE
D25. This has always been strong for war. ~ FORT
D26. What one of the ex-doughboys did to a big steak dinner right after the bonus bill was passed. ~ ORDERED
D28. She might have come from Galveston. ~ GAL
D30. Getting up at 6 A. M. on a cold winter morning. ~ TASK
D34. You will be going some when you start on this. ~ TRIP
D36. Too much of this makes people fat. ~ EASE
D38. An overhead expense in grandmother's day. ~ EGRET
D39. The way pedestrians get through the traffic. ~ MAIMED
D40. What the early bird did to the warm muffins. ~ ATE
D41. These make about ten revolutions a minute. ~ REDS
D43. A weighty matter in India. ~ SER
D44. We have defined this before in this puzzle. ~ ERE
D45. This is not "so long" in Florida as it is in Cuba. ~ ADIOS
D47. The "jack" of all trades. ~ DOLLAR
D48. Their first deposit on an expensive oriental rug. ~ ASHES
D54. Shure and this little word is what started O'Flaherty. ~ OF
D57. Medicine man. ~ DOC
D59. You can take all the time you wish for this one. ~ ERA
D60. You may be sure there are no angles to this. ~ ARC
D61. You may come across this sometime in the midst of your travels (abbr.). ~ AVE
D62. A secretary's byword. ~ PER
D64. A backward man. ~ EH
D66. A red-haired feminine pronoun. ~ IT


